// New articles, 14 November 2026 (part 3): animals, everyday life, food, work and sport.
ADDART({id:"polarbear",t:"Polar Bears",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐻‍❄️",w:"Polar bear",r:["arctic","bear","climchange","inuit","tundra"],
p:[["Arctic hunters","Polar bears are the largest land carnivores, living on the sea ice of the Arctic."],["Built for cold","Thick fur, black skin and a layer of fat keep them warm in freezing conditions."],["Melting ice","Climate change is shrinking the sea ice they hunt from."]],
l:"The polar bear is a large bear that lives in the Arctic, on the frozen sea around the North Pole and the coasts of Canada, Greenland, Norway, Russia and Alaska. Adult males can weigh 400 to 600 kilograms, making polar bears the largest land carnivores, alongside the Kodiak brown bear. They are superbly adapted to the cold, with two layers of fur over black skin, a thick layer of fat, small ears and large furry paws that spread their weight on ice and help them swim. Polar bears hunt mainly seals, waiting at breathing holes in the sea ice. Because the ice is melting earlier and forming later as the climate warms, polar bears have less time to hunt, and they are listed as vulnerable, with an estimated 22,000 to 31,000 left in the wild.",
s:[["Life on the ice","Polar bears depend on sea ice as a platform for hunting ringed and bearded seals, their main food. A bear may wait motionless for hours beside a seal’s breathing hole. In summer, when the ice melts in many areas, bears come ashore and may go months with little food, living off their fat. They are strong swimmers and have been tracked swimming for days."],["Fur and skin","Polar bear fur looks white but is actually made of transparent, hollow hairs that scatter light. Underneath, the skin is black, which absorbs warmth from the sun. A layer of fat up to 10 centimeters thick insulates the bear so well that it can overheat when running."],["Mothers and cubs","Pregnant females dig dens in snowdrifts in autumn and give birth to one to three tiny cubs, each weighing about 600 grams, in midwinter. The family stays in the den until spring, when the cubs are big enough to follow their mother onto the ice. Cubs stay with her for about two and a half years, learning to hunt."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Ursus maritimus"],["Weight (males)","400–600 kg"],["Main food","Seals"],["Population","About 22,000–31,000"],["Status","Vulnerable"]],
d:[["c.150,000 years ago","Polar bears split from brown bears (estimates vary)"],["1973","Arctic countries agree to protect polar bears"],["2008","Listed as threatened in the United States"],["2015","Listed as vulnerable on the IUCN Red List"],["2020s","Sea ice reaches record lows in several years"]],
s2:[["Churchill, the polar bear capital","Each autumn, hundreds of polar bears gather near the town of Churchill in Manitoba, Canada, waiting for Hudson Bay to freeze. Visitors watch them from large ‘tundra buggies’, and the town has a ‘polar bear jail’ where bears that wander into town are held until they can be released onto the ice."],["Shrinking ice","Satellite records show Arctic summer sea ice has declined sharply since 1979. In Hudson Bay, the ice-free season is now several weeks longer than in the 1980s, and the local polar bear population has fallen as bears spend longer on shore without food."]],
sp:["Polar bear versus brown bear","Polar bears evolved from brown bears and can still breed with them; a few hybrids, nicknamed ‘pizzly’ or ‘grolar’ bears, have been found in the wild. Polar bears have longer necks, smaller heads and larger paws, and unlike brown bears they eat almost nothing but meat."],
g:[["Carnivore","An animal that eats mainly meat"],["Sea ice","Frozen seawater floating on the ocean"],["Den","A sheltered place where an animal rests or gives birth"],["Vulnerable","At risk of becoming endangered"]],
f:["Polar bear skin is black.","A polar bear’s fat layer can be 10 centimeters thick.","Newborn cubs weigh only about 600 grams."],
fun:"Polar bears are so well insulated that they are almost invisible to infrared cameras."});

ADDART({id:"honeybees",t:"Honeybees",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐝",w:"Honey bee",r:["insect","flower","agri","wasps","ants"],
p:[["Makers of honey","Honeybees collect nectar from flowers and turn it into honey."],["Life in the hive","A colony has one queen, thousands of female workers and some male drones."],["Pollinators","Bees pollinate many of the crops people eat."]],
l:"Honeybees are social insects that live in large colonies and make honey from the nectar of flowers. The best-known species, the western honeybee, is kept by beekeepers around the world. A colony has one queen, who lays all the eggs, up to tens of thousands of female workers, who gather food, build wax comb, care for the young and defend the hive, and a few hundred male drones, whose only role is to mate with new queens. Workers tell each other where flowers are by performing a ‘waggle dance’. As they move from flower to flower, honeybees carry pollen, pollinating many crops, including apples, almonds, berries and cucumbers. Honeybees and wild bees face threats from pesticides, parasites, disease and habitat loss.",
s:[["Inside the hive","Bees build comb from wax produced by glands on their bodies, forming six-sided cells that store honey and pollen and hold the eggs and larvae. The hive is kept at about 35°C; bees fan their wings to cool it and cluster together to keep warm in winter. A strong summer colony can have 50,000 or more bees."],["Making honey","Worker bees suck nectar from flowers and store it in a special honey stomach. Back at the hive, they pass it to other bees, who add enzymes and spread it in cells, fanning it until most of the water evaporates. The thick honey is sealed with wax. A colony may visit millions of flowers to make a single kilogram of honey."],["The waggle dance","When a worker finds good flowers, she dances on the comb in a figure of eight, waggling her body. The angle of the dance tells other bees the direction of the flowers relative to the sun, and the length of the waggle tells them the distance. Austrian scientist Karl von Frisch decoded the dance and won a Nobel Prize in 1973."]],
k:[["Best-known species","Western honeybee (Apis mellifera)"],["Colony","One queen, thousands of workers, some drones"],["Hive temperature","About 35°C"],["Communication","The waggle dance"],["Stings","Workers die after stinging a mammal"]],
d:[["c.7000 BC","Rock art in Spain shows honey gathering"],["c.2400 BC","Egyptians keep bees in clay hives"],["1851","Langstroth invents the movable-frame hive"],["1973","Karl von Frisch wins a Nobel Prize for decoding bee dances"],["2006","Beekeepers report colony collapse disorder"],["2018","World Bee Day first celebrated on 20 May"]],
s2:[["A royal diet","All female larvae start the same, but those fed only royal jelly, a rich secretion from worker bees, develop into queens. A queen lives for several years and can lay over 1,500 eggs a day, while workers in summer live only about six weeks."],["Bees in trouble","Since the 2000s, beekeepers in many countries have reported heavy losses of colonies. Causes include the varroa mite, a parasite that spreads viruses, certain pesticides, poor nutrition from loss of wild flowers, and disease. Planting flowers and limiting pesticides help both honeybees and wild bees."]],
sp:["Why a bee dies when it stings","A honeybee worker’s sting is barbed. When she stings a person or animal with thick skin, the sting stays behind, tearing away part of her body, so she dies. Queens have smooth stings and can sting repeatedly. Drones have no sting at all."],
g:[["Nectar","A sweet liquid made by flowers"],["Pollen","Powder from flowers that fertilizes other flowers"],["Colony","A group of animals living together"],["Drone","A male bee"]],
f:["A bee colony may visit millions of flowers to make a kilogram of honey.","A queen can lay over 1,500 eggs a day.","Honey found in Egyptian tombs was still edible after thousands of years."],
fun:"Honeybees can recognize human faces in experiments."});

ADDART({id:"newspapers",t:"Newspapers",c:"The Modern World",i:"📰",w:"Newspaper",r:["journalism","print","misinformation","socialmedia","libraries"],
p:[["Daily news in print","Newspapers are printed publications of news, opinion and advertising."],["Four centuries old","The first regular printed newspapers appeared in Europe in the early 1600s."],["Going digital","Many newspapers now reach more readers online than in print."]],
l:"A newspaper is a publication, usually printed on large sheets of inexpensive paper and issued daily or weekly, containing news, articles, opinion, photographs, advertisements and features such as puzzles and comics. The first regular printed newspapers appeared in German cities around 1605. Cheap printing, the telegraph and rising literacy made newspapers a mass medium in the 19th century, and for over a century they were the main way people learned about the world. Since the 1990s, the internet has taken away readers and advertising, and many newspapers have closed or shrunk, while others have built large online audiences. Newspapers remain important for investigative reporting and holding the powerful to account.",
s:[["Early newspapers","Handwritten news sheets circulated in ancient Rome and in China, where court gazettes were produced for officials. The printed newspaper began in Europe: Johann Carolus’s ‘Relation’ in Strasbourg, from about 1605, is often called the first. Early papers were small and heavily censored by governments."],["The age of the press","In the 1800s, steam-powered presses, cheaper paper and the telegraph let newspapers print tens of thousands of copies with news from around the world. ‘Penny papers’ sold for a coin, and press barons such as Joseph Pulitzer and William Randolph Hearst built huge circulations with bold headlines and sensational stories."],["Newspapers today","Printed circulation has fallen steeply in most countries since 2000, while websites, apps and podcasts have grown. Many papers now charge for online subscriptions. Thousands of local newspapers have closed, leaving ‘news deserts’, and journalists face pressure, misinformation and, in some countries, danger. India and Japan still have some of the world’s largest print circulations."]],
k:[["First printed newspaper","About 1605, Strasbourg"],["Largest circulation","Yomiuri Shimbun, Japan"],["Key inventions","Steam press, telegraph"],["Famous prize","Pulitzer Prize, since 1917"],["Press Freedom Day","3 May"]],
d:[["c.1605","Johann Carolus prints the ‘Relation’"],["1702","The first daily newspaper in English, London"],["1833","The New York Sun launches the penny press"],["1844","The telegraph speeds up news"],["1917","The first Pulitzer Prizes"],["1990s","Newspapers go online"]],
s2:[["The Watergate investigation","In 1972–74, two reporters at The Washington Post, Bob Woodward and Carl Bernstein, investigated a break-in at the Democratic Party headquarters in the Watergate building. Their reporting helped uncover a cover-up that led to the resignation of US President Richard Nixon in 1974, a landmark of investigative journalism."],["Reading the front page","Newspapers put the most important stories on the front page, with the biggest headline ‘above the fold’, the top half seen when the paper is folded on a newsstand. Inside, sections cover national and world news, business, sport, culture and opinion, including editorials that give the paper’s own view."]],
sp:["Broadsheets and tabloids","Newspapers come in two main sizes. Broadsheets are large and traditionally more serious; tabloids are half the size and often use bold headlines and pictures. The word tabloid has also come to mean sensational journalism, though many tabloid-sized papers are serious."],
g:[["Circulation","The number of copies a newspaper sells"],["Editorial","An article giving the newspaper’s opinion"],["Headline","The title of a news story"],["Byline","The line naming the writer of an article"]],
f:["The first printed newspaper appeared around 1605.","The telegraph let newspapers report distant events within hours.","World Press Freedom Day is 3 May."],
fun:"The crossword puzzle first appeared in a newspaper, the New York World, in 1913."});

ADDART({id:"postal",t:"The Postal Service",c:"The Modern World",i:"✉️",w:"Mail",r:["writing","persia","journalism","ecommerce","ships"],
p:[["Carrying messages","Postal services carry letters and parcels from sender to receiver."],["Ancient relays","Persian and Roman empires had relay riders carrying official messages."],["The postage stamp","The first stamp, the Penny Black, appeared in Britain in 1840."]],
l:"A postal service is a system for collecting, transporting and delivering letters, documents and parcels. Ancient empires, including Persia, Rome, China and the Inca, ran relay systems of riders or runners to carry official messages across great distances, but for most of history ordinary people had no way to send letters. Public postal services spread in Europe from the 1600s. In 1840, Britain introduced the Penny Black, the first adhesive postage stamp, and a low flat rate paid by the sender, a model copied worldwide. The Universal Postal Union, founded in 1874, lets mail flow between countries. Today, letter volumes are falling because of email and messaging, but parcel deliveries have soared with online shopping.",
s:[["Ancient messengers","The Persian king Darius I set up a relay of mounted couriers along the Royal Road around 500 BC; the Greek historian Herodotus wrote that neither snow, rain, heat nor darkness stopped them, words now carved on a New York post office. The Romans had the cursus publicus, and the Inca used runners called chasquis who passed messages along mountain roads."],["The postal revolution","Before 1840, postage in Britain was expensive and usually paid by the receiver, based on distance. Rowland Hill proposed a cheap uniform rate paid in advance with a stamp. The Penny Black, bearing Queen Victoria’s head, let anyone send a letter anywhere in the country for one penny. Letter writing exploded, and other countries soon adopted stamps."],["Mail today","Postal services use sorting machines that read addresses and postcodes, and move mail by truck, train and plane. Many national posts also offer banking and government services. With fewer letters, posts have shifted to parcels, competing with private delivery companies. Letters still matter for official documents and for people without internet access."]],
k:[["First stamp","Penny Black, Britain, 1840"],["World body","Universal Postal Union, 1874"],["Ancient relay","Persian Royal Road, c.500 BC"],["Postcodes","Introduced from the 1940s–60s"],["Trend","Fewer letters, more parcels"]],
d:[["c.500 BC","Persian relay couriers on the Royal Road"],["1516","Henry VIII appoints a Master of the Posts in England"],["1840","The Penny Black is issued"],["1860–61","The Pony Express crosses the American West"],["1874","The Universal Postal Union is founded"],["1911","First official airmail flight, in India"]],
s2:[["The Pony Express","For 18 months in 1860–61, riders of the Pony Express carried mail about 3,000 kilometers between Missouri and California in around ten days, changing horses every 15 to 25 kilometers. The completion of the transcontinental telegraph put it out of business, but it became a legend of the American West."],["Postcodes","To help sorting, countries introduced postal codes: Germany in 1941, the United Kingdom from 1959 and the United States in 1963 with the ZIP code. Codes let machines sort millions of items a day and direct them to the right town, street or even building."]],
sp:["Stamp collecting","Collecting stamps, called philately, became a popular hobby soon after stamps appeared. Rare stamps with printing errors can be worth millions; the British Guiana 1c magenta, the only one of its kind, sold for over 8 million US dollars in 2021."],
g:[["Postage","The fee for sending mail"],["Courier","A person who carries messages or packages"],["Sorting office","A place where mail is sorted for delivery"],["Philately","Stamp collecting"]],
f:["The Penny Black was the world’s first adhesive postage stamp.","The Universal Postal Union was founded in 1874.","The rarest stamp sold for over 8 million US dollars."],
fun:"Before stamps, the person receiving a letter usually had to pay for it."});

ADDART({id:"dyes",t:"Dyes and Color",c:"Technology",i:"🎨",w:"Dye",r:["cotton","clothing","phoenicians","reactions","light"],
p:[["Coloring the world","Dyes are substances that give lasting color to cloth, leather, paper and food."],["Precious purples","Some ancient dyes were worth more than gold."],["The synthetic revolution","In 1856, a teenager accidentally made the first synthetic dye."]],
l:"A dye is a colored substance that bonds to a material, such as cotton, wool, silk, leather or paper, giving it a lasting color. For thousands of years, dyes came from plants, animals and minerals: blue from the indigo plant, red from madder roots and from cochineal insects, yellow from saffron and turmeric, and the famous Tyrian purple from sea snails. Many dyes needed a mordant, often a metal salt, to fix them to the cloth. In 1856, William Henry Perkin, an 18-year-old English chemist, accidentally created mauve, the first synthetic dye, from coal tar. Synthetic dyes soon replaced most natural ones, making bright colors cheap. Today, dyeing textiles is a huge industry, and untreated dye waste is a major source of water pollution.",
s:[["Natural dyes","Indigo, grown in India, Africa and the Americas, gives the deep blue of jeans. Madder gave the red of soldiers’ coats, and cochineal, made from crushed insects on cacti in Mexico, produced brilliant scarlet, prized by the Aztecs and later Europe. Tyrian purple, made by the Phoenicians from thousands of murex snails per gram, was so costly that it was reserved for emperors."],["Perkin’s mauve","In 1856, William Perkin was trying to make the malaria medicine quinine from coal tar when he noticed a purple residue. He found that it dyed silk a lovely mauve that did not fade. He opened a factory, and ‘mauve mania’ swept fashion. Chemists went on to create thousands of synthetic dyes, founding the modern chemical industry."],["Dyeing today","Modern dyes are designed to bond to particular fibers, such as reactive dyes for cotton and disperse dyes for polyester. The textile industry is one of the world’s largest users of water and chemicals, and rivers near dyeing factories in some countries run in the colors of the season’s fashions. Cleaner dyeing methods and recycled water are slowly spreading."]],
k:[["Oldest dyes","Plant and mineral dyes, over 5,000 years old"],["Famous ancient dye","Tyrian purple, from sea snails"],["First synthetic dye","Mauve, 1856"],["Blue of jeans","Indigo"],["Fixer","Mordant"]],
d:[["c.2500 BC","Indigo-dyed cloth in the Indus Valley and Peru"],["c.1500 BC","Phoenicians make Tyrian purple"],["1500s","Cochineal is shipped from Mexico to Europe"],["1856","Perkin discovers mauve"],["1897","Synthetic indigo is produced commercially"],["1900s","Thousands of synthetic dyes replace natural ones"]],
s2:[["Why jeans fade","Indigo does not soak into cotton fibers but sits on their surface. Each wash and rub removes a little dye, so jeans fade gradually at the knees and seams, the worn look many people like. Traditional indigo vats are fermented, and the cloth turns from yellow-green to blue as it meets the air."],["Colors and status","Because some dyes were so expensive, colors became signs of rank. Roman law limited purple to the emperor, Chinese emperors claimed yellow, and in medieval Europe, laws called sumptuary laws controlled who could wear scarlet. Cheap synthetic dyes ended these distinctions."]],
sp:["Dyes and pigments","Dyes dissolve and bond chemically to the material they color, while pigments are tiny insoluble particles held on a surface by a binder, as in paint and ink. The same substance can sometimes be used as either."],
g:[["Mordant","A substance that fixes a dye to a fiber"],["Indigo","A plant and the blue dye made from it"],["Synthetic","Made by people using chemistry"],["Pigment","A colored powder used in paints and inks"]],
f:["Tyrian purple needed thousands of snails per gram.","The first synthetic dye was discovered by an 18-year-old.","Indigo gives jeans their blue color."],
fun:"The red dye cochineal, made from insects, is still used to color some foods and cosmetics."});

ADDART({id:"umbrellas",t:"Umbrellas",c:"How We Live",i:"☂️",w:"Umbrella",r:["monsoon","clothing","chinacountry","storms","phtyph"],
p:[["Shelter you can carry","An umbrella is a folding canopy on a handle that protects against rain or sun."],["Ancient sunshades","The first umbrellas, over 3,000 years ago, were for shade and status."],["Everyday object","Billions are made each year, most of them in one Chinese city."]],
l:"An umbrella is a folding canopy supported by ribs on a central pole, held by hand to protect a person from rain or sunlight. The word comes from the Latin umbra, meaning shade, and umbrellas used against the sun are also called parasols. Umbrellas appeared more than 3,000 years ago in ancient Egypt, Assyria, China and India, where servants held them over rulers as a sign of power. Waterproof umbrellas of oiled paper or silk were used in China, and by the 18th century umbrellas were common in Europe, though men were at first mocked for carrying them. The folding steel-ribbed umbrella of the 1850s and the compact collapsible umbrella of the 20th century made them cheap and portable. Today, most of the world’s umbrellas are made in Shangyu, in China.",
s:[["Symbols of power","In ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia, carvings show attendants shading kings with umbrellas. In Southeast Asia, tiered umbrellas were symbols of royalty and are still used in ceremonies in Thailand, Cambodia and Myanmar. In Buddhism, the parasol is one of the eight auspicious symbols, representing protection."],["Keeping off the rain","The Chinese were making waterproof umbrellas by coating paper or silk with oil or lacquer over 1,000 years ago, and oiled-paper umbrellas remain a traditional craft in China and Japan. In Europe, umbrellas became fashionable in the 1700s; the Englishman Jonas Hanway is credited with making it acceptable for men to carry one in London in the 1750s."],["Modern umbrellas","In 1852, Samuel Fox invented a lightweight frame of steel ribs, and in 1928 Hans Haupt patented a telescopic folding umbrella. Today’s umbrellas use fiberglass ribs and nylon or polyester canopies. Over a billion are sold each year, and many are thrown away after breaking in the wind, creating waste."]],
k:[["Word origin","Latin umbra, ‘shade’"],["Oldest use","Over 3,000 years ago, as sunshades"],["Steel frame","1852, Samuel Fox"],["Folding umbrella","1928"],["Main producer","Shangyu, China"]],
d:[["c.1000 BC","Umbrellas shade rulers in Egypt and Assyria"],["c.AD 1000","Waterproof oiled-paper umbrellas in China"],["1750s","Umbrellas become accepted for men in London"],["1852","The steel-ribbed frame is invented"],["1928","The telescopic folding umbrella"],["1960s","Cheap nylon umbrellas spread worldwide"]],
s2:[["Wagasa","Traditional Japanese umbrellas, called wagasa, are made from bamboo and washi paper coated with oil, with dozens of thin ribs. They were once used daily and are now mostly used in tea ceremonies, dance and festivals. A few workshops in Gifu and Kyoto still make them by hand."],["Umbrellas in the tropics","In hot, rainy countries, umbrellas are carried all year, for shade in the sun and shelter in the monsoon. In parts of Southeast Asia, umbrella vendors appear on streets the moment rain begins, and umbrellas are sold at nearly every corner shop."]],
sp:["Why umbrellas turn inside out","In a strong gust, wind catches under the canopy and pushes it upward. If the force is greater than the ribs can resist, they bend backwards and the canopy flips inside out. Vented umbrellas have a second layer that lets gusts escape, and some modern designs are tested in wind tunnels."],
g:[["Canopy","The cover of an umbrella"],["Rib","One of the thin rods supporting the canopy"],["Parasol","An umbrella used for shade from the sun"],["Telescopic","Able to slide into itself to become shorter"]],
f:["The word umbrella comes from the Latin word for shade.","The Chinese made waterproof umbrellas over 1,000 years ago.","Most of the world’s umbrellas come from one city in China."],
fun:"The parasol is one of the eight lucky symbols of Buddhism."});

ADDART({id:"pizza",t:"Pizza",c:"How We Live",i:"🍕",w:"Pizza",r:["italycountry","wheat","cheese","potato","migration"],
p:[["A dish from Naples","Pizza is a flatbread topped with tomato, cheese and other ingredients, baked in a hot oven."],["Protected tradition","Neapolitan pizza-making is on UNESCO’s list of intangible heritage."],["Eaten everywhere","Billions of pizzas are eaten each year around the world."]],
l:"Pizza is a dish of flattened bread dough topped with tomato sauce, cheese and other ingredients, baked at high temperature. Flatbreads with toppings have been eaten since ancient times, but modern pizza was born in Naples, Italy, in the 18th and 19th centuries, as cheap street food for the city’s poor, after tomatoes arrived from the Americas. Italian emigrants carried pizza to the United States, where it became hugely popular after the Second World War and developed new styles, such as thick Chicago deep-dish pizza. Today pizza is one of the most popular foods in the world, from traditional wood-fired Neapolitan pizza to frozen pizzas and delivery chains. In 2017, UNESCO added the art of the Neapolitan pizza maker, the pizzaiuolo, to its list of intangible cultural heritage.",
s:[["Naples","In 18th-century Naples, bakers sold flatbreads topped with lard, cheese, garlic or tomatoes to workers, who ate them folded in the street. The pizza Margherita, with tomato, mozzarella and basil, is said to have been created in 1889 for Queen Margherita of Italy in the colors of the Italian flag, though the combination existed earlier."],["Pizza goes to America","Italian immigrants opened pizzerias in New York from 1905. After American soldiers returned from Italy in 1945 with a taste for pizza, it spread across the country. Chicago developed deep-dish pizza in the 1940s, and chains that began in the 1950s and 60s turned pizza into a global delivery business."],["Styles around the world","True Neapolitan pizza has a soft, puffy crust and is baked in about 90 seconds in a wood-fired oven at around 485°C. Roman pizza is thin and crisp. In Japan, pizza may be topped with squid or mayonnaise; in Brazil, with green peas or bananas; in Sweden, with kebab meat. Pineapple topping, invented in Canada in 1962, is famously debated."]],
k:[["Origin","Naples, Italy"],["Classic","Pizza Margherita, 1889"],["Oven temperature","About 485°C (Neapolitan)"],["UNESCO heritage","Neapolitan pizza-making, 2017"],["Hawaiian pizza invented","Canada, 1962"]],
d:[["1700s","Flatbreads with toppings sold in Naples"],["1889","The Margherita pizza is named"],["1905","An early pizzeria opens in New York"],["1945","Returning soldiers spread pizza across the United States"],["1962","Pineapple pizza is invented in Canada"],["2017","Neapolitan pizza-making listed by UNESCO"]],
s2:[["Rules for Neapolitan pizza","The Associazione Verace Pizza Napoletana sets rules for true Neapolitan pizza: the dough must be made of flour, water, salt and yeast, stretched by hand, never rolled; the pizza must be no more than 35 centimeters across with a raised edge, and baked in a wood-fired oven for 60 to 90 seconds."],["Pizza in space","In 2001, a pizza was delivered to the International Space Station, and in 2017 astronauts assembled their own pizzas in orbit from tortilla bases, sauce and toppings, floating them in front of the cameras before eating."]],
sp:["The pizza box","The square cardboard box with a lid, used for delivering round pizzas, was developed in the 1960s. Vents let steam escape so the crust does not go soggy, and the small plastic table in the middle stops the lid from sticking to the cheese."],
g:[["Pizzaiuolo","A pizza maker, especially in Naples"],["Mozzarella","A soft Italian cheese"],["Wood-fired oven","An oven heated by burning wood"],["Deep-dish","A thick pizza baked in a deep pan"]],
f:["Neapolitan pizza bakes in about 90 seconds.","The Margherita is said to show the colors of the Italian flag.","Pizza has been eaten on the International Space Station."],
fun:"Americans eat about 3 billion pizzas a year."});

ADDART({id:"curry",t:"Curry",c:"How We Live",i:"🍛",w:"Curry",r:["spice","indiacountry","japancountry","migration","rice"],
p:[["Spiced dishes","Curry is a general name for spiced dishes of meat, fish or vegetables in a sauce."],["A traveling word","The word spread from India through the British Empire to the world."],["Many national versions","Thai, Japanese, Caribbean and British curries are all different."]],
l:"Curry is a word used, especially outside South Asia, for a wide range of dishes made with a sauce seasoned with spices such as turmeric, cumin, coriander, ginger, chili and garlic. In India, Pakistan, Bangladesh and Sri Lanka, people usually call their dishes by specific names, such as korma, rogan josh or dal, rather than curry; the English word probably comes from the Tamil kari, meaning sauce or relish, picked up by Portuguese and British traders. Spiced dishes spread with trade and empire, and curry became a national favorite in Britain and Japan and developed distinct forms in Thailand, Malaysia, the Caribbean, South Africa and elsewhere. Chicken tikka masala, created in Britain, has been called a British national dish.",
s:[["Spices of South Asia","Archaeologists have found traces of turmeric, ginger and garlic in cooking pots from the Indus Valley Civilization over 4,000 years ago. Indian cooks developed blends of spices, called masalas, toasted and ground fresh. Chilies, now central to many curries, arrived from the Americas with Portuguese traders in the 16th century."],["Curry travels","British officials returning from India brought a taste for spiced dishes, and ‘curry powder’, a ready-made blend, was sold in Britain from the 18th century. Indian sailors and migrants opened restaurants in Britain, and ‘going for a curry’ became part of British life. Indentured workers carried curries to the Caribbean, Fiji and South Africa."],["Curries of Asia","Thai curries are made with pastes of chilies, lemongrass, galangal and shrimp paste, cooked in coconut milk; green, red and massaman are famous. Japanese curry, introduced by the British navy in the 19th century, is a thick, mild stew served with rice and is one of Japan’s most popular meals. Malaysian and Indonesian rendang is a slow-cooked dry curry."]],
k:[["Word origin","Probably Tamil kari, ‘sauce’"],["Key spices","Turmeric, cumin, coriander, chili"],["Chilies arrived","1500s, from the Americas"],["British favorite","Chicken tikka masala"],["Japanese favorite","Kare raisu (curry rice)"]],
d:[["c.2500 BC","Spices used in Indus Valley cooking"],["1500s","Portuguese bring chilies to India"],["1747","An early curry recipe is printed in England"],["1810","An Indian restaurant opens in London"],["1870s","The British navy introduces curry to Japan"],["2001","A British minister calls chicken tikka masala a national dish"]],
s2:[["Turmeric","Turmeric, a root related to ginger, gives many curries their golden color. It has been used in India for thousands of years in cooking, medicine and ceremonies, and is used to dye the robes of some Buddhist monks."],["Rendang","Rendang, from the Minangkabau people of Sumatra, Indonesia, is beef slowly cooked for hours in coconut milk and spices until the liquid is gone and the meat is dark and tender. Originally made to preserve meat without refrigeration, it has been voted one of the world’s most delicious foods."]],
sp:["Curry houses","Britain has thousands of ‘Indian’ restaurants, many run by families from Bangladesh. Dishes such as balti, from Birmingham, and chicken tikka masala were created in Britain for British tastes. The curry industry employs around 100,000 people there."],
g:[["Masala","A blend of spices"],["Turmeric","A yellow spice made from a root"],["Coconut milk","A liquid made from grated coconut"],["Indentured worker","A person bound to work for a set time, often far from home"]],
f:["Chilies were unknown in India until the 1500s.","Japanese curry came from the British navy.","Turmeric gives curry its yellow color."],
fun:"Japanese schools often serve curry rice for lunch, and the navy traditionally eats it every Friday."});

ADDART({id:"icecream",t:"Ice Cream",c:"How We Live",i:"🍦",w:"Ice cream",r:["refrigeration","cheese","sugar","choco","molecules"],
p:[["A frozen treat","Ice cream is a sweet frozen food made from milk or cream, sugar and flavorings."],["Royal luxury","Before refrigerators, only the rich could enjoy frozen desserts."],["A global favorite","People eat billions of liters of ice cream every year."]],
l:"Ice cream is a frozen dessert made from cream or milk, sugar and flavorings such as vanilla, chocolate or fruit, churned while freezing so that tiny ice crystals and air bubbles give it a smooth, light texture. Flavored ices made with snow and fruit syrups were enjoyed in ancient Persia, China and Rome, and frozen cream desserts appeared in Italy and France in the 17th century. For a long time ice cream was a luxury, because ice had to be cut from frozen lakes and stored in ice houses. Hand-cranked freezers in the 1840s and mechanical refrigeration in the 20th century made it cheap and available to everyone. Today, many kinds are popular, including Italian gelato, Indian kulfi, soft serve and dairy-free ices.",
s:[["Early ices","Around 500 BC, Persians cooled fruit syrups with ice stored in yakhchals, dome-shaped ice houses. Chinese records from the Tang dynasty describe a frozen dish of milk and flour. In 16th- and 17th-century Italy, cooks learned that salt lowers the freezing point of ice, allowing them to freeze creams; the dessert spread to the French court."],["Ice cream for everyone","In 1843, American Nancy Johnson patented a hand-cranked ice cream freezer, and in 1851 Jacob Fussell opened the first ice cream factory. Ice cream cones became popular at the 1904 World’s Fair in St Louis. Electric freezers in homes and shops in the 20th century turned ice cream into an everyday treat."],["How it is made","Ice cream mix is pasteurized, cooled and churned as it freezes, which breaks up ice crystals and whips in air; the amount of air, called overrun, can double the volume. Gelato is churned more slowly with less air and served slightly warmer, which makes its flavor more intense. Sugar and fat keep ice cream soft enough to scoop."]],
k:[["Main ingredients","Cream or milk, sugar, flavoring"],["Key science","Salt lowers the freezing point of ice"],["Hand-cranked freezer","1843"],["Cone popularized","1904 World’s Fair"],["Biggest eaters per person","New Zealand, United States, Australia"]],
d:[["c.500 BC","Persians make iced fruit syrups"],["1600s","Frozen cream desserts in Italy and France"],["1843","Nancy Johnson patents the hand-cranked freezer"],["1851","The first ice cream factory"],["1904","Ice cream cones at the St Louis World’s Fair"],["1930s","Soft-serve ice cream is developed"]],
s2:[["Brain freeze","Eating ice cream too fast can cause a sudden sharp headache, often called brain freeze. The cold touches the roof of the mouth, and blood vessels nearby quickly narrow and widen, which nerves report as pain in the forehead. It passes in less than a minute."],["Kulfi and dondurma","Kulfi, from the Indian subcontinent, is made by slowly boiling milk until it thickens, then freezing it in molds without churning, giving a dense, creamy ice. Turkish dondurma contains salep, a flour made from orchid roots, and mastic resin, making it so stretchy and chewy that vendors perform tricks with it."]],
sp:["Why salt freezes ice cream","Mixing salt with ice lowers the temperature at which the ice melts, to well below 0°C. The melting ice draws heat from the cream mixture beside it, freezing the cream. This trick, known in Italy by the 1500s, is still used in hand-cranked ice cream makers."],
g:[["Churn","To stir a mixture continuously"],["Overrun","The amount of air whipped into ice cream"],["Gelato","Italian-style ice cream with less air"],["Pasteurize","To heat food to kill germs"]],
f:["Persians were eating flavored ices about 2,500 years ago.","Gelato has less air than regular ice cream.","Turkish dondurma is stretchy because it contains orchid-root flour."],
fun:"Vanilla is the most popular ice cream flavor in most countries."});

ADDART({id:"mirrors",t:"Mirrors",c:"Science",i:"🪞",w:"Mirror",r:["light","glass","scope","illusions","camera"],
p:[["Reflecting light","A mirror is a smooth surface that reflects light to form an image."],["From polished metal to silvered glass","Early mirrors were polished stone or bronze; glass mirrors came later."],["Science and telescopes","Curved mirrors gather light in the world’s largest telescopes."]],
l:"A mirror is an object with a smooth, shiny surface that reflects light so clearly that it forms an image of whatever is in front of it. The earliest mirrors, around 8,000 years ago in Anatolia, were polished pieces of volcanic glass called obsidian; later people used polished bronze, copper and silver. Glass mirrors backed with a thin layer of metal were perfected in Venice in the 16th century and were enormously expensive. In 1835, German chemist Justus von Liebig developed a way to coat glass with a thin film of silver, making mirrors cheap. Flat mirrors reverse images front to back; curved mirrors can magnify or shrink them, and large concave mirrors gather light in telescopes, including the James Webb Space Telescope.",
s:[["How mirrors work","Light bounces off a mirror at the same angle at which it arrives, a rule called the law of reflection. Because the surface is so smooth, all the rays stay organized and form a clear image. The image in a flat mirror appears as far behind the mirror as the object is in front, and it is reversed front to back, which is why writing appears backwards."],["A history of mirrors","Obsidian mirrors have been found at Çatalhöyük in Türkiye from around 6000 BC. Egyptians and Chinese used polished bronze. Venetian craftsmen on Murano made glass mirrors backed with tin and mercury; in the 1600s, a Venetian mirror could cost more than a painting by a famous artist. Liebig’s silvering process of 1835 and later aluminum coatings made mirrors ordinary household objects."],["Curved mirrors","Concave mirrors, curved inward, can focus light and magnify, which is why they are used in shaving mirrors, car headlights and reflecting telescopes. Convex mirrors, curved outward, show a wider view in a smaller image, useful for car side mirrors and shop security. The James Webb Space Telescope’s main mirror is 6.5 meters across, made of 18 gold-coated hexagonal segments."]],
k:[["Oldest mirrors","Obsidian, about 8,000 years ago"],["Glass mirrors perfected","Venice, 1500s"],["Silvering process","Justus von Liebig, 1835"],["Largest space mirror","James Webb, 6.5 m"],["Law of reflection","Angle in equals angle out"]],
d:[["c.6000 BC","Obsidian mirrors at Çatalhöyük"],["c.2900 BC","Polished copper mirrors in Egypt"],["1500s","Venetian glass mirrors"],["1668","Isaac Newton builds a reflecting telescope"],["1835","Liebig invents silvered glass mirrors"],["2021","The James Webb Space Telescope is launched"]],
s2:[["The Hall of Mirrors","The Hall of Mirrors at the Palace of Versailles, completed in 1684, has 357 mirrors facing 17 windows onto the gardens. At the time, mirrors were so costly that the hall was a display of France’s wealth; the Treaty of Versailles ending the First World War was signed there in 1919."],["Animals and mirrors","Scientists test whether animals recognize themselves by putting a mark on them and showing them a mirror. Great apes, dolphins, elephants and magpies have passed the test, touching or examining the mark. Most animals treat the reflection as another animal."]],
sp:["One-way mirrors","A so-called one-way mirror is a sheet of glass with a very thin reflective coating. It works only when one side is brightly lit and the other dark: people in the bright room see their reflection, while people in the dark room see through. If the lights are switched, the effect reverses."],
g:[["Reflection","Light bouncing off a surface"],["Concave","Curved inward, like the inside of a spoon"],["Convex","Curved outward, like the back of a spoon"],["Silvering","Coating glass with a thin layer of metal"]],
f:["The earliest mirrors were made of volcanic glass.","Venetian mirrors once cost more than famous paintings.","The James Webb Space Telescope’s mirror is coated with gold."],
fun:"Ambulances often have their name written backwards on the front so drivers can read it in their rear-view mirrors."});

ADDART({id:"zoos",t:"Zoos",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦁",w:"Zoo",r:["conservation","extinction","museums","lion","panda"],
p:[["Animals on display","Zoos keep wild animals for people to see, study and protect."],["From menageries to conservation","Modern zoos focus on breeding endangered species and education."],["A continuing debate","People disagree about whether keeping animals in zoos is right."]],
l:"A zoo, short for zoological garden, is a place where wild animals are kept, displayed to the public, studied and, increasingly, bred to help save endangered species. Rulers kept collections of exotic animals, called menageries, in ancient Egypt, China and Mesopotamia, and the Aztec emperor Moctezuma had a famous one. The first modern public zoos opened in Vienna in 1752 and London in 1828, where scientists studied animals in cages. During the 20th century, zoos replaced bars with larger, more natural enclosures and began coordinated breeding programs. Zoos have helped save species such as the California condor, the Arabian oryx and Przewalski’s horse from extinction. Critics argue that many animals, especially large and intelligent ones, suffer in captivity.",
s:[["From menagerie to zoo","The Vienna zoo at Schönbrunn, opened to the public in 1752, is the oldest zoo still operating. London Zoo opened in 1828 for scientific study and to the public in 1847; the word ‘zoo’ comes from its name, the Zoological Gardens. In 1907, Carl Hagenbeck in Hamburg built the first enclosures with moats instead of bars, so animals appeared to roam free."],["Saving species","Breeding programs link zoos around the world, moving animals between them to keep populations healthy. The Arabian oryx, extinct in the wild by 1972, was bred in zoos and returned to the deserts of Oman and Saudi Arabia. California condors, down to 27 birds in 1987, now number over 500 thanks to zoo breeding. Zoos also fund conservation in the wild."],["The debate","Animal welfare groups point to elephants, big cats and whales showing signs of stress in zoos, and many countries have ended the keeping of orcas and dolphins for shows. Supporters say good zoos give animals space and enrichment, educate millions of visitors and are a last refuge for species losing their habitats. Standards vary widely between zoos."]],
k:[["Oldest zoo","Schönbrunn, Vienna, 1752"],["Word origin","London’s Zoological Gardens"],["First moated enclosures","Hamburg, 1907"],["Species saved","Arabian oryx, California condor"],["Visitors worldwide","Over 700 million a year"]],
d:[["c.1500 BC","Queen Hatshepsut keeps exotic animals in Egypt"],["1752","Schönbrunn zoo opens in Vienna"],["1828","London Zoo is founded"],["1907","Hagenbeck opens bar-free enclosures"],["1982","Arabian oryx returned to the wild"],["1990s–","Many zoos end elephant and orca shows"]],
s2:[["Giant pandas abroad","China lends giant pandas to zoos around the world, a practice sometimes called panda diplomacy. Zoos pay large fees that support conservation in China, and cubs born abroad belong to China and usually return there. Pandas draw huge crowds wherever they go."],["Safari parks and sanctuaries","Safari parks let animals roam large areas while visitors drive through. Sanctuaries care for rescued animals and do not breed or display them for entertainment. Some zoos have become ‘conservation parks’, giving more space to fewer species."]],
sp:["Enrichment","Modern zoos give animals enrichment: puzzles that hide food, new scents and objects, climbing structures and pools, to keep them active and prevent boredom. Keepers also train animals with rewards so that medical checks can be done without stress."],
g:[["Menagerie","A collection of wild animals kept for show"],["Enclosure","The area in which a zoo animal lives"],["Breeding program","A plan to breed animals to save a species"],["Captivity","Being kept and unable to live in the wild"]],
f:["The word zoo comes from London’s Zoological Gardens.","Vienna’s zoo has operated since 1752.","California condors were saved by zoo breeding."],
fun:"Baby pandas born in zoos abroad are sent back to China when they are a few years old."});

ADDART({id:"hospitals",t:"Hospitals",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🏥",w:"Hospital",r:["nightingale","antibiotics","telemedicine","who","hygiene"],
p:[["Places of healing","Hospitals are institutions where sick and injured people receive medical care."],["Ancient roots","Early hospitals were run by temples, monasteries and Islamic rulers."],["Modern medicine","Today’s hospitals combine doctors, nurses, laboratories and advanced machines."]],
l:"A hospital is an institution that provides medical and surgical treatment and nursing care for sick or injured people, with doctors, nurses and other staff working around the clock. The earliest hospital-like institutions included healing temples in ancient Greece, hospitals in Sri Lanka and India more than 2,000 years ago, and the bimaristans of the medieval Islamic world, which treated patients free of charge. In Europe, monasteries and charities ran hospitals for the poor. Modern hospitals developed in the 18th and 19th centuries, transformed by the discoveries of germs, anesthesia and antiseptics and by Florence Nightingale’s reforms of nursing. Today, hospitals range from small rural clinics to huge university medical centers with thousands of beds.",
s:[["Early hospitals","King Ashoka of India is said to have built hospitals in the 3rd century BC, and ruins of a hospital from around the 9th century survive at Mihintale in Sri Lanka. In Baghdad, Cairo and Damascus from the 9th century, bimaristans had wards for different diseases, pharmacies and medical schools. Christian monasteries in Europe cared for the sick and travelers."],["The modern hospital","Until the 1800s, hospitals were often dangerous places where infections spread. The discovery that germs cause disease, Joseph Lister’s antiseptic surgery from 1867 and Nightingale’s insistence on clean, well-ventilated wards changed that. Anesthesia, X-rays from 1895 and antibiotics in the 1940s made hospitals centers of effective treatment rather than last resorts."],["Hospitals today","A large hospital has an emergency department, operating theaters, intensive care units, maternity wards, laboratories, imaging departments with scanners, and pharmacies. Many are also teaching and research centers. Access to hospital care varies greatly between rich and poor countries, and the COVID-19 pandemic showed how quickly hospitals can be overwhelmed."]],
k:[["Early examples","Bimaristans of the Islamic world, 800s"],["Oldest surviving hospital remains","Mihintale, Sri Lanka"],["Antiseptic surgery","Joseph Lister, 1867"],["Nursing reforms","Florence Nightingale, 1850s"],["Largest hospitals","Several thousand beds"]],
d:[["c.250 BC","Ashoka builds hospitals in India"],["805","A bimaristan is founded in Baghdad"],["1123","St Bartholomew’s Hospital is founded in London"],["1846","First public demonstration of surgical anesthesia"],["1867","Lister introduces antiseptic surgery"],["1948","Britain’s National Health Service begins"]],
s2:[["The emergency department","Emergency departments treat patients with sudden illnesses and injuries. Nurses first assess how urgent each case is, a process called triage, so the most serious patients are seen first. Ambulances, often with paramedics who begin treatment on the way, bring the most critical cases."],["Hospital ships","Some hospitals float. Hospital ships, such as the Mercy Ships that visit African ports and navy ships that respond to disasters, carry operating theaters and wards to places where hospitals are damaged or far away."]],
sp:["Why hospitals are white","Hospitals adopted white walls, uniforms and sheets in the late 1800s because white shows dirt easily and can be boiled clean, part of the new emphasis on hygiene. Today, many hospitals use colors and artwork to make wards calmer, especially for children."],
g:[["Ward","A room or area in a hospital for patients"],["Triage","Sorting patients by how urgent their condition is"],["Antiseptic","A substance that kills germs on skin or surfaces"],["Intensive care","Treatment for the most seriously ill patients"]],
f:["Hospitals in the medieval Islamic world treated patients for free.","X-rays were first used in hospitals in 1896.","Triage comes from a French word meaning ‘sorting’."],
fun:"The word hospital comes from the Latin hospes, meaning guest, the same root as hotel and hospitality."});

ADDART({id:"firefighters",t:"Firefighters",c:"How We Live",i:"🚒",w:"Firefighter",r:["disasterprep","volunteering","police","hospitals","thermo","wildfires"],
p:[["Fighting fires, saving lives","Firefighters put out fires and rescue people from burning buildings, crashes and floods."],["Roman beginnings","Ancient Rome had a corps of 7,000 firefighters called the Vigiles."],["Prevention too","Modern fire services also inspect buildings and teach fire safety."]],
l:"Firefighters are trained workers, paid or volunteer, who put out fires and rescue people and animals from danger. Besides fires, they respond to road accidents, floods, chemical spills, collapsed buildings and medical emergencies, and they inspect buildings and teach fire safety. The first organized fire brigade is often said to be the Vigiles of ancient Rome, set up by Emperor Augustus in AD 6. In early modern cities, insurance companies ran private brigades that protected only buildings carrying their mark. Public fire services spread in the 19th century, with steam-powered pumps, horse-drawn engines and later motor fire engines. Today’s firefighters use protective suits, breathing apparatus, thermal cameras and ladder trucks, and wildfire crews fight fires across whole landscapes.",
s:[["History","Before pumps, people fought fires with bucket chains, passing water hand to hand from wells and rivers. After the Great Fire of London in 1666, insurance companies set up brigades, and the first public fire departments appeared in the 18th and 19th centuries. The fire hydrant, hose and steam pump greatly improved firefighting, and the first motorized fire engines appeared around 1900."],["How fires are fought","Fire needs heat, fuel and oxygen. Firefighters remove one of these: cooling the fire with water, smothering it with foam or cutting off its fuel. They enter burning buildings in protective gear with air tanks, searching for people and ventilating smoke. Commanders plan the attack and track every crew for safety."],["Wildfires and rescue","Wildland firefighters dig firebreaks, set controlled burns and are supported by aircraft dropping water and retardant. Specialist teams rescue people trapped under rubble after earthquakes, from floodwater and from heights. In many countries, most firefighters are volunteers who train regularly and respond from their homes and workplaces."]],
k:[["Early brigade","Roman Vigiles, AD 6"],["Fire needs","Heat, fuel and oxygen"],["Key tools","Hoses, ladders, breathing apparatus"],["Motor fire engines","From about 1900"],["Many firefighters are","Volunteers"]],
d:[["AD 6","Augustus founds the Vigiles in Rome"],["1666","The Great Fire of London leads to insurance brigades"],["1725","Richard Newsham patents an improved fire pump"],["1829","The first steam fire engine"],["1900s","Motorized fire engines replace horses"],["1970s","Women join professional fire services in many countries"]],
s2:[["The fire pole","In 1878, a Chicago firefighter installed a wooden pole so crews could slide quickly from their upstairs quarters to the engines below, saving precious seconds. Brass poles soon spread to fire stations worldwide, though many modern stations are built on one level."],["Smoke alarms","Most fire deaths are caused by smoke, often while people sleep. Smoke alarms, cheap and widely available since the 1970s, detect smoke and sound a loud warning. Firefighters recommend testing them regularly and planning an escape route."]],
sp:["Firefighters’ gear","A firefighter’s protective clothing, called turnout gear, is made of heat-resistant layers and weighs around 20 kilograms with helmet, boots and the air tank that lets them breathe in smoke. A thermal imaging camera shows heat through smoke, helping them find people and hidden flames."],
g:[["Fire engine","A vehicle carrying pumps, hoses and equipment"],["Hydrant","A pipe in the street that supplies water for fighting fires"],["Firebreak","A gap in vegetation that stops a wildfire spreading"],["Breathing apparatus","Equipment that supplies clean air in smoke"]],
f:["Ancient Rome had about 7,000 firefighters.","A firefighter’s gear weighs around 20 kilograms.","The fire pole was invented in Chicago in 1878."],
fun:"Dalmatian dogs became fire-station mascots because they once ran alongside horse-drawn fire engines, calming the horses."});

ADDART({id:"police",t:"The Police",c:"Society",i:"👮",w:"Police",r:["law","democ","hrights","firefighters","journalism"],
p:[["Keeping order","Police forces enforce the law, prevent crime and protect people."],["Modern policing","The first modern police force was founded in London in 1829."],["Trust and debate","How police should act and be held accountable is debated in many countries."]],
l:"The police are an organized force whose job is to enforce laws, keep public order, prevent and investigate crime, and protect people and property. Ancient cities had watchmen and officials who kept order, but the first modern, uniformed, professional police force was the Metropolitan Police in London, founded by Robert Peel in 1829; British officers are still nicknamed ‘bobbies’ after him. Peel’s principles held that police should prevent crime, use force only as a last resort and depend on the public’s approval. Today, police forces exist in every country and include patrol officers, detectives, traffic police and specialist units. How police are trained, armed and held accountable, and how they treat different communities, are important debates in many societies.",
s:[["Before modern police","In ancient Rome, the Vigiles patrolled at night, and in medieval Europe towns relied on constables and citizens taking turns as night watchmen. In 1667, Paris created a lieutenant general of police, and in the 18th century London had the Bow Street Runners, a small group of paid thief-takers."],["Peel’s police","Robert Peel’s Metropolitan Police wore blue uniforms rather than military red, carried only wooden truncheons and patrolled on foot in set areas called beats. Peel’s nine principles stressed that the police are the public and the public are the police. Other cities soon followed: New York founded its police department in 1845."],["Police today","Modern police use radios, databases, forensic science, CCTV and body-worn cameras. Many countries have national and local forces. Police powers are limited by law, and independent bodies investigate complaints. Concerns about the use of force, racial bias and surveillance have led to reforms and protests in many places, while others worry about rising crime."]],
k:[["First modern force","Metropolitan Police, London, 1829"],["Founder","Robert Peel"],["Nickname","Bobbies (UK)"],["Emergency numbers","911, 112, 999 and others"],["International body","Interpol, founded 1923"]],
d:[["1667","Paris creates a police lieutenant general"],["1829","The Metropolitan Police is founded in London"],["1845","New York City forms its police department"],["1901","Scotland Yard begins using fingerprints"],["1923","Interpol is founded"],["2010s","Body-worn cameras spread"]],
s2:[["Fingerprints","In 1901, London’s Scotland Yard began identifying suspects by fingerprints, which are unique to each person. Fingerprinting replaced earlier systems of body measurements and remains a basic tool of police work, now alongside DNA analysis, introduced in the 1980s."],["Community policing","Community policing encourages officers to work closely with residents, walking beats, attending local meetings and solving problems together, rather than only responding to calls. Supporters say it builds trust and prevents crime; it requires time and steady relationships."]],
sp:["Interpol","Interpol, based in Lyon, France, helps police forces in 196 countries share information and track suspects across borders. It issues ‘red notices’ asking countries to arrest wanted people, but it has no officers of its own and cannot make arrests."],
g:[["Detective","A police officer who investigates crimes"],["Beat","The area an officer regularly patrols"],["Forensic science","Using science to investigate crimes"],["Accountability","Being answerable for one’s actions"]],
f:["British police are called bobbies after Robert Peel.","Scotland Yard began using fingerprints in 1901.","Interpol has member countries but no officers of its own."],
fun:"Early London police were told to be polite and were forbidden from carrying guns, a rule that mostly continues today."});

ADDART({id:"universities",t:"Universities",c:"Society",i:"🎓",w:"University",r:["libraries","education","onlinelearning","goldage","medieurope"],
p:[["Higher learning","Universities teach advanced subjects and carry out research."],["Nearly a thousand years old","The oldest universities date from the Middle Ages, in Africa, the Middle East and Europe."],["Degrees and research","Students earn degrees, and researchers make discoveries that change the world."]],
l:"A university is an institution of higher education and research that teaches a wide range of subjects and awards degrees, from bachelor’s to doctorates. The University of al-Qarawiyyin in Fez, Morocco, founded in 859, and al-Azhar in Cairo, founded in the 970s, are among the oldest institutions of higher learning, while the University of Bologna in Italy, founded in 1088, is the oldest in continuous operation in Europe. Medieval universities taught law, medicine, theology and the arts. Universities expanded enormously in the 19th and 20th centuries, adding sciences and engineering and opening to women and to students from all backgrounds. Today more than 200 million students are enrolled in higher education worldwide, and universities are centers of research in medicine, technology and every field of knowledge.",
s:[["Origins","Centers of advanced learning existed in ancient India at Nalanda, in Alexandria and in Chinese imperial academies. The medieval university emerged as a self-governing community of teachers and students; the word comes from the Latin universitas, meaning a guild or corporation. Bologna, Paris and Oxford set the pattern for Europe, with lectures, examinations and degrees."],["Opening up","For centuries, universities were small and mostly for wealthy men. In the 1800s, Germany’s research universities combined teaching with original research, a model copied worldwide. Women were admitted from the late 19th century. After 1945, governments expanded universities massively, and in many countries nearly half of young people now attend."],["Universities today","Universities grant bachelor’s degrees after about three or four years, master’s degrees and doctorates for original research. They run libraries, laboratories and hospitals and spin off companies. Rankings compare them, though many question the measures. The cost of tuition and student debt are major issues in some countries, while others offer free or low-cost study."]],
k:[["Oldest in operation","Al-Qarawiyyin, Fez, 859"],["Oldest in Europe","Bologna, 1088"],["Word origin","Latin universitas, ‘guild’"],["Students worldwide","Over 200 million"],["Highest degree","Doctorate (PhD)"]],
d:[["859","Al-Qarawiyyin is founded in Fez"],["1088","The University of Bologna is founded"],["c.1096","Teaching begins at Oxford"],["1810","The University of Berlin starts the research university model"],["1869","Women are admitted to universities in Britain"],["2012","Massive open online courses (MOOCs) spread"]],
s2:[["Fatima al-Fihri","Al-Qarawiyyin in Fez was founded in 859 by Fatima al-Fihri, a woman who used her inheritance to build a mosque with a center of learning attached. It grew into a university teaching religion, law, mathematics and astronomy and still operates today, recognized by UNESCO and Guinness World Records as the oldest existing university."],["Caps and gowns","The gowns and square caps worn at graduation ceremonies come from the clothing of medieval scholars, who were often clergy and dressed warmly in unheated halls. Colors on hoods and gowns show the degree and field of study."]],
sp:["The oldest university in the Philippines","The University of Santo Tomas in Manila, founded in 1611, is the oldest existing university in Asia, older than Harvard, the oldest in the United States, which was founded in 1636."],
g:[["Degree","A qualification awarded by a university"],["Lecture","A talk given to teach a subject"],["Doctorate","The highest university degree, based on research"],["Tuition","The fee paid for teaching"]],
f:["Al-Qarawiyyin was founded by a woman, Fatima al-Fihri, in 859.","The University of Bologna dates from 1088.","Over 200 million students study at universities worldwide."],
fun:"The word university originally meant a guild, like those of merchants and craftsmen."});

ADDART({id:"weddings",t:"Weddings",c:"How We Live",i:"💍",w:"Wedding",r:["festivals","hindu","christ","islam","clothing"],
p:[["Celebrating marriage","A wedding is the ceremony in which two people are married."],["Many traditions","Rings, vows, feasts and special clothes vary from culture to culture."],["Old customs, new forms","Weddings today blend ancient customs with personal choices."]],
l:"A wedding is a ceremony in which two people are joined in marriage, usually followed by a celebration with family and friends. Wedding customs differ enormously around the world but often include vows or promises, the exchange of rings or gifts, special clothing, blessings by religious or civil officials, music, dancing and a feast. In many cultures, weddings also join two families and involve gifts between them. Some traditions are very old: the exchange of rings goes back to ancient Egypt and Rome, and white wedding dresses became popular in the West after Queen Victoria wore one in 1840. In recent decades, many countries have legalized civil weddings, interfaith marriages and same-sex marriages.",
s:[["Ceremonies","Christian weddings usually take place in a church with vows and rings; Jewish weddings are held under a canopy called a chuppah and end with the breaking of a glass; Muslim weddings center on the signing of a marriage contract, the nikah; Hindu weddings may last days and include the couple walking seven times around a sacred fire. Civil weddings before an official are common everywhere."],["Clothes and colors","White dresses are traditional in the West, but red is the color of luck and joy for brides in China and India, and brides in many African countries wear brightly patterned cloth. Grooms may wear suits, traditional robes or, in Scotland, kilts. In Japan, a bride may change outfits several times during the day."],["Feasts and customs","Wedding feasts range from small meals to celebrations for hundreds of guests lasting several days. Customs include throwing rice or petals, cutting a cake, first dances, gifts of money in envelopes, and games and speeches. Some customs are meant to bring luck or fertility; others are simply fun."]],
k:[["Common elements","Vows, rings, feast"],["White dress popularized","Queen Victoria, 1840"],["Lucky color in Asia","Red"],["Jewish canopy","Chuppah"],["Hindu ritual","Seven steps around a fire"]],
d:[["c.2000 BC","Marriage contracts in Mesopotamia"],["Ancient Rome","Rings exchanged as a sign of marriage"],["1840","Queen Victoria’s white wedding dress"],["1900s","Civil marriage spreads worldwide"],["2001","The Netherlands legalizes same-sex marriage, the first country to do so"],["2020","Many weddings move online during the pandemic"]],
s2:[["The ring finger","Wedding rings are often worn on the fourth finger of the left hand because the ancient Romans believed a vein ran from that finger directly to the heart, the ‘vena amoris’. In some countries, including Germany, Russia and India, rings are worn on the right hand."],["Mass weddings","In some countries, including the Philippines, South Korea and India, local governments and organizations hold mass weddings in which dozens or hundreds of couples marry at once, often to help couples who cannot afford a ceremony of their own."]],
sp:["Henna night","Before weddings in South Asia, the Middle East and North Africa, women gather for a henna or mehndi party, where the bride’s hands and feet are decorated with intricate patterns in henna paste that stain the skin reddish-brown for weeks. The evening is filled with music, dancing and food."],
g:[["Vow","A solemn promise"],["Ceremony","A formal event with set actions"],["Civil wedding","A marriage performed by a government official"],["Henna","A plant dye used to decorate skin"]],
f:["White wedding dresses became popular after 1840.","Red is the traditional wedding color in China and India.","Wedding rings go back to ancient Egypt and Rome."],
fun:"At Jewish weddings, the groom breaks a glass underfoot, and guests shout ‘Mazel tov!’, meaning good luck."});

ADDART({id:"pets",t:"Pets",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐾",w:"Pet",r:["dogs","cats","horses","fish","rabbits"],
p:[["Animal companions","Pets are animals kept for company and enjoyment rather than work or food."],["An ancient bond","Dogs were the first animals to live with humans, over 15,000 years ago."],["Good for us","Pets can improve people’s mood, health and sense of responsibility."]],
l:"A pet is an animal kept mainly for companionship, pleasure or protection, rather than for work or food. Dogs and cats are the most popular pets worldwide, followed by fish, birds, rabbits, hamsters, guinea pigs and reptiles. Dogs were the first domesticated animals, living with hunter-gatherers more than 15,000 years ago, and cats began living near people when farming created stores of grain that attracted mice. Keeping animals purely as companions was once mostly for the wealthy, but pet-keeping grew enormously in the 20th century. Studies suggest that caring for pets can reduce stress and loneliness and increase exercise. Pets also need food, veterinary care, exercise and attention, and abandoned or stray animals are a serious problem in many places.",
s:[["Dogs and cats","Dogs descend from wolves and were bred over thousands of years into hundreds of breeds for herding, hunting, guarding and companionship. Cats were sacred in ancient Egypt and spread around the world on ships, where they controlled rats. Today there are estimated to be around 900 million dogs and 600 million cats in the world, though many are not pets."],["Other pets","Goldfish have been kept in China for over a thousand years. Budgerigars, canaries and parrots are popular birds. Rabbits, guinea pigs and hamsters suit small homes, and many people keep reptiles, such as geckos and tortoises. Exotic pets, such as monkeys and big cats, often suffer in captivity, and trade in them can threaten wild populations."],["Caring for pets","Pets need the right food, clean water, space to exercise, shelter and regular visits to a veterinarian for vaccinations and check-ups. Many countries require dogs to be licensed or microchipped, and animal welfare laws protect pets from cruelty. Neutering helps control numbers of unwanted animals, and shelters find homes for strays."]],
k:[["Most popular pets","Dogs and cats"],["First domesticated","Dogs, over 15,000 years ago"],["Dogs worldwide","About 900 million"],["Cats worldwide","About 600 million"],["Benefits","Companionship, exercise, lower stress"]],
d:[["c.15,000 years ago","Dogs live with humans"],["c.9,500 years ago","A cat is buried with a person in Cyprus"],["c.1000","Goldfish kept in ponds in China"],["1800s","Pet-keeping spreads among the middle classes"],["1866","The ASPCA is founded in the United States"],["2000s","Pet ownership rises worldwide"]],
s2:[["Hachikō","Hachikō was a dog in Tokyo who met his owner, a professor, at Shibuya station every day. After the professor died suddenly in 1925, Hachikō kept returning to the station every day for nearly ten years until his own death. A statue of him at the station is a popular meeting place and a symbol of loyalty."],["Pets and health","Research suggests that stroking a pet can lower blood pressure and that dog owners walk more than people without dogs. Therapy animals visit hospitals, care homes and schools, and assistance dogs help people who are blind, deaf or have other disabilities."]],
sp:["Choosing a pet","Different animals suit different homes. Dogs need daily walks and company; cats are more independent; fish and small animals need less space but still need daily care. Some pets live a long time: parrots and tortoises can outlive their owners."],
g:[["Domesticated","Tamed and bred to live with people"],["Veterinarian","A doctor for animals"],["Microchip","A tiny implant that identifies a pet"],["Neuter","To operate on an animal so it cannot breed"]],
f:["Dogs were domesticated over 15,000 years ago.","A cat was buried with a person in Cyprus about 9,500 years ago.","Some parrots can live for over 60 years."],
fun:"Cats cannot taste sweetness."});

ADDART({id:"rabbits",t:"Rabbits and Hares",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐇",w:"Rabbit",r:["pets","domestication","grasslands","invasiveplants","australiacountry"],
p:[["Long ears, strong legs","Rabbits and hares are small mammals known for long ears, big back legs and fast breeding."],["Burrows and forms","Rabbits dig burrows and live in groups; hares live alone above ground."],["Pets and pests","Rabbits are popular pets but became a plague in Australia."]],
l:"Rabbits and hares are small mammals of the family Leporidae, with long ears, large hind legs for hopping and running, big eyes set on the sides of the head and constantly growing front teeth for gnawing plants. Rabbits are born blind and hairless in underground burrows and often live in groups; hares are born furred with open eyes in a shallow nest on the ground and live mostly alone. The European rabbit, native to Spain and Portugal, has been spread by people around the world and is the ancestor of all domestic rabbits, which are kept as pets and for meat and fur. Rabbits breed very quickly, and in Australia, where they were introduced in 1859, they became one of the most damaging invasive species.",
s:[["Built to escape","Rabbits and hares are prey for foxes, eagles, snakes and many other animals, so they are built to notice danger and flee. Their eyes see almost all the way around, their ears turn to catch sounds, and their powerful hind legs let hares reach about 70 kilometers per hour. Rabbits thump the ground with a hind foot to warn others."],["Life in a warren","European rabbits dig networks of burrows called warrens, with many entrances, where they shelter and raise their young. A female can have several litters a year of up to a dozen kits each. Rabbits eat grass and other plants and digest it twice, re-eating special soft droppings to extract more nutrients."],["Rabbits and people","Romans kept rabbits in walled enclosures, and monks in medieval France bred them. Domestic rabbits come in over 300 breeds, from tiny dwarfs to giants weighing over 7 kilograms. In Australia, 24 rabbits released for hunting in 1859 multiplied into hundreds of millions, stripping grasslands; fences thousands of kilometers long and diseases introduced to control them have had mixed results."]],
k:[["Family","Leporidae"],["Fastest hare","About 70 km/h"],["Litters","Several a year, up to 12 kits"],["Domestic breeds","Over 300"],["Invasive in","Australia, since 1859"]],
d:[["c.1000 BC","Phoenicians find rabbits in Spain"],["Roman times","Rabbits kept in enclosures"],["1500s","Monks breed domestic rabbits in France"],["1859","24 rabbits released in Australia"],["1901–07","Australia builds rabbit-proof fences"],["1950","Myxomatosis released to control Australian rabbits"]],
s2:[["The rabbit-proof fence","Between 1901 and 1907, Western Australia built three fences totaling more than 3,000 kilometers to keep rabbits out of farmland. The longest, at 1,833 kilometers, was then the longest unbroken fence in the world. Rabbits still got through, but the fences became part of Australian history."],["Rabbits in culture","Rabbits and hares appear in stories worldwide: the trickster Br’er Rabbit, the March Hare in ‘Alice in Wonderland’, Peter Rabbit, Bugs Bunny and the Easter Bunny. In East Asian folklore, a rabbit lives on the Moon, pounding rice cakes or medicine; the Rabbit is one of the twelve animals of the zodiac."]],
sp:["Not rodents","Rabbits and hares look a little like large rodents and were once classified with them, but they belong to a separate order, the lagomorphs, together with the pikas of mountains. Unlike rodents, they have a second pair of small teeth behind their upper front teeth."],
g:[["Warren","A network of rabbit burrows"],["Kit","A young rabbit"],["Leveret","A young hare"],["Invasive species","An introduced species that harms its new environment"]],
f:["Hares can run at about 70 kilometers per hour.","Rabbits digest their food twice.","Australia’s rabbit-proof fence was over 1,800 kilometers long."],
fun:"A rabbit’s teeth never stop growing, so it must gnaw constantly to wear them down."});

ADDART({id:"fencing",t:"Fencing",c:"How We Live",i:"🤺",w:"Fencing",r:["olymp","martial","samurai","castles","gymnastics"],
p:[["Sword fighting as sport","Fencing is a sport in which two competitors fight with blunted swords."],["Three weapons","Fencers use the foil, the épée or the sabre, each with its own rules."],["Electric scoring","Hits are recorded electronically through wires in the fencers’ clothing."]],
l:"Fencing is a combat sport in which two competitors try to score points by touching each other with a sword while avoiding being touched. It developed from the swordsmanship taught for dueling and warfare in Europe, and schools of fencing flourished in Spain, Italy and France from the 15th century. Modern fencing uses three weapons: the foil, a light thrusting weapon that scores only on the torso; the épée, a heavier thrusting weapon that scores anywhere on the body; and the sabre, a cutting and thrusting weapon that scores above the waist. Bouts take place on a narrow strip called a piste, and hits are recorded electronically. Fencing has been part of every modern Olympic Games since 1896, and women’s events were added from 1924.",
s:[["From duels to sport","In the 1500s and 1600s, gentlemen settled disputes with duels, and fencing masters taught the rapier and later the smallsword. Protective masks, invented around 1780, made it safe to practice at full speed, turning fencing into a sport. Italy and France developed rival styles, and the first international rules were agreed in the early 1900s."],["The three weapons","In foil and sabre, the rule of ‘right of way’ decides who scores when both fencers hit at the same time: the fencer who attacked first, or who defended by parrying, gets the point. In épée there is no right of way; if both hit within a fraction of a second, both score. Fencers wear a mask, padded jacket, glove and, in foil and sabre, a conductive vest."],["Fencing today","A bout is fought to 15 touches in elimination rounds, on a piste 14 meters long and up to 2 meters wide. Italy, France and Hungary have long been powers, joined by Russia, South Korea, the United States and others. Wheelchair fencing is part of the Paralympic Games."]],
k:[["Weapons","Foil, épée, sabre"],["Olympic sport","Since 1896"],["Playing area","Piste, 14 m long"],["Bout","To 15 touches (elimination)"],["Scoring","Electronic"]],
d:[["1400s","Fencing schools in Spain, Italy and Germany"],["c.1780","The protective wire mask is invented"],["1896","Fencing at the first modern Olympics"],["1913","The international fencing federation is founded"],["1936","Electric scoring introduced for épée"],["1960","Wheelchair fencing at the first Paralympics"]],
s2:[["Why fencers say ‘touché’","Touché, French for ‘touched’, is what a fencer traditionally says to acknowledge being hit by an opponent. Fencing’s language is largely French, with terms such as en garde (on guard), allez (go) and riposte (a counter-attack after a parry), because France shaped the sport’s rules."],["Electric fencing","Since the 1930s, fencers have been wired to a scoring box. In foil and sabre, a conductive jacket covers the target area, and a hit completes a circuit that lights a lamp. In épée, the point of the sword has a button that must be pressed with enough force to register."]],
sp:["Footwork","Fencers move up and down the piste with quick steps, advances, retreats and lunges, in which the front leg shoots forward to reach the opponent. A fleche, French for arrow, is a running attack. Good footwork is as important as blade work."],
g:[["Piste","The strip on which fencers compete"],["Parry","Blocking an attack with the blade"],["Riposte","A counter-attack after a parry"],["Lunge","A long step forward to reach the opponent"]],
f:["Fencing has been in every modern Olympic Games.","The protective mask was invented around 1780.","Fencing’s language is mostly French."],
fun:"Hungary won the Olympic men’s sabre title at every Games from 1908 to 1964."});

ADDART({id:"rowing",t:"Rowing",c:"How We Live",i:"🚣",w:"Rowing (sport)",r:["ships","olymp","rivers","universities","viking"],
p:[["Boats moved by oars","Rowing is moving a boat through water with oars."],["From transport to racing","Rowing races began among watermen and university students in the 1700s and 1800s."],["A test of teamwork","Crews of up to eight must pull in perfect time."]],
l:"Rowing is the act of propelling a boat with oars, and as a sport it means racing long, narrow boats called shells. Rowers sit facing backwards and pull on oars fixed to the boat, using their legs, backs and arms. In sweep rowing, each rower has one oar; in sculling, each has two. Boats carry one, two, four or eight rowers, and larger boats often have a coxswain, who steers and calls the rhythm. Rowing was a form of transport and warfare for thousands of years, from Greek triremes to Viking longships. Racing developed in the 1700s among the watermen of London and in the 1800s among university students; the Oxford–Cambridge Boat Race began in 1829. Rowing has been an Olympic sport since 1900, and races are usually 2,000 meters long.",
s:[["Oars through history","Ancient Egyptians rowed on the Nile, and Greek triremes, warships with three rows of oars, won the Battle of Salamis in 480 BC. Viking longships combined sails and oars. Rowing galleys were used in the Mediterranean until the 1700s. Rowing boats remained everyday transport in cities built on rivers and canals."],["The sport","The first recorded modern race, Doggett’s Coat and Badge, has been rowed on the Thames by London watermen since 1715. Universities took up racing in the 1800s, and the sliding seat, introduced in the 1870s, let rowers use their legs for power. Rowing joined the Olympics in 1900, and women’s events were added in 1976."],["How crews row","A stroke has four parts: the catch, when the blade enters the water; the drive, pushing with the legs, then swinging the back and pulling with the arms; the finish, when the blade comes out; and the recovery, sliding forward for the next stroke. In an eight, all rowers must match the timing of the stroke rower, with the coxswain steering and guiding the race."]],
k:[["Boat","Shell"],["Types","Sweep (one oar) and sculling (two)"],["Crew sizes","1, 2, 4 or 8"],["Olympic distance","2,000 m"],["Oldest race","Doggett’s Coat and Badge, since 1715"]],
d:[["480 BC","Greek triremes win at Salamis"],["1715","Doggett’s Coat and Badge race begins"],["1829","The first Oxford–Cambridge Boat Race"],["1870s","The sliding seat is introduced"],["1900","Rowing at the Olympics"],["1976","Women’s Olympic rowing events"]],
s2:[["The Boat Race","The Boat Race between Oxford and Cambridge universities, first held in 1829 and annually since 1856, is rowed over about 6.8 kilometers on the River Thames in London. Hundreds of thousands watch from the banks and millions on television. A women’s race has been held since 1927 and on the same course since 2015."],["Steve Redgrave","British rower Steve Redgrave won gold medals at five consecutive Olympic Games from 1984 to 2000, one of the greatest achievements in endurance sport. After his fourth gold in 1996 he said anyone who saw him near a boat again could shoot him, then came back to win a fifth."]],
sp:["Dragon boats","Dragon boat racing, from southern China, uses long boats paddled by crews of around 20, with a drummer setting the pace and a dragon’s head on the bow. Races are held at the Dragon Boat Festival in early summer and are now popular worldwide. Paddlers face forward, unlike rowers."],
g:[["Oar","A long pole with a blade used to move a boat"],["Coxswain","The person who steers a rowing boat and directs the crew"],["Sculling","Rowing with two oars, one in each hand"],["Stroke","One complete cycle of the oar, or the rower who sets the rhythm"]],
f:["Rowers face backwards.","Olympic rowing races are 2,000 meters long.","The Boat Race has been held since 1829."],
fun:"In an eight, the coxswain is the only person who can see where the boat is going."});

ADDART({id:"sailing",t:"Sailing",c:"How We Live",i:"⛵",w:"Sailing",r:["ships","wind","explor","polynesia","olymp"],
p:[["Powered by the wind","Sailing uses the wind on sails to move a boat across the water."],["Thousands of years of voyages","Sailing boats carried traders and explorers across the oceans."],["Sport and recreation","Today sailing is a popular pastime and an Olympic sport."]],
l:"Sailing is the art of moving a boat or ship across water using the wind on sails. People have sailed for at least 5,000 years: Egyptians sailed the Nile, Polynesians crossed the Pacific in double-hulled canoes, and Arab, Chinese and European sailors built ships that carried trade, exploration and empire across the world’s oceans. A sailing boat can travel in almost any direction except straight into the wind, by angling its sails and zigzagging upwind, a technique called tacking. Steamships replaced sailing ships for most trade in the 19th century, but sailing survived as sport and recreation. Yacht racing ranges from small dinghies to the America’s Cup and round-the-world races, and sailing has been an Olympic sport since 1900.",
s:[["Ships of the past","Egyptian boats around 3000 BC had a single square sail. Arab dhows and Pacific canoes used triangular sails that could sail closer to the wind. Chinese junks had battened sails and watertight compartments. European caravels and later clippers, the fastest sailing cargo ships, carried tea and wool across oceans in the 1800s before steam took over."],["How sails work","When the wind blows straight from behind, it simply pushes the sail. But a sail angled to the wind works like an airplane wing: air flowing over its curved surface creates a force that pulls the boat forward and sideways, while the keel or centerboard under the hull resists the sideways push. By sailing at an angle to the wind and turning, or tacking, boats can make progress upwind."],["Sailing as sport","Yacht clubs formed in the 1700s and 1800s, and the America’s Cup, first raced in 1851, is the oldest international sporting trophy. Olympic sailing uses standard classes of boats, from one-person dinghies to windsurfers and kiteboards. Solo sailors race around the world non-stop in the Vendée Globe, taking around 70 to 80 days."]],
k:[["Oldest evidence","Egypt, about 3000 BC"],["Key technique","Tacking to sail upwind"],["Oldest trophy","America’s Cup, 1851"],["Olympic sport","Since 1900"],["Round-the-world race","Vendée Globe"]],
d:[["c.3000 BC","Sailing boats on the Nile"],["c.1000","Polynesians reach distant Pacific islands"],["1400s–1500s","European voyages of exploration"],["1851","The first America’s Cup race"],["1900","Sailing at the Olympics"],["1968–69","Robin Knox-Johnston sails solo around the world non-stop"]],
s2:[["The America’s Cup","In 1851, the yacht America beat British boats in a race around the Isle of Wight and took home a silver trophy that became the America’s Cup. American yachts held it for 132 years, the longest winning streak in sport, until Australia won in 1983. Today’s Cup boats are high-tech foiling yachts that lift out of the water."],["Navigating by the stars","Before instruments, sailors found their way by the sun, stars, winds, currents and birds. Polynesian navigators memorized star paths and read ocean swells; later, the compass, sextant and chronometer let sailors fix their position. Today, satellite navigation makes it easy, but sailors still learn traditional skills."]],
sp:["Parts of a sailing boat","The hull is the body of the boat; the mast holds up the sails; the mainsail is the largest sail and the jib a smaller one in front; the boom is the pole along the bottom of the mainsail; the rudder steers; and the keel or centerboard under the hull keeps the boat from sliding sideways and tipping over."],
g:[["Tacking","Turning the bow through the wind to sail a zigzag course upwind"],["Keel","A fin under a boat that keeps it stable"],["Dinghy","A small open sailing boat"],["Yacht","A boat used for racing or pleasure"]],
f:["Sailing boats cannot sail straight into the wind.","The America’s Cup is the oldest international sporting trophy.","Clipper ships were the fastest sailing cargo ships."],
fun:"Modern America’s Cup yachts rise out of the water on foils and can sail faster than the wind."});

ADDART({id:"horseracing",t:"Horse Racing",c:"How We Live",i:"🏇",w:"Horse racing",r:["horses","olymp","prob","ukcountry","mongol"],
p:[["The sport of kings","Horse racing is the racing of horses ridden by jockeys over a set course."],["An ancient sport","Chariot and horse races were held in ancient Greece and Rome."],["Famous races","The Kentucky Derby, the Grand National and the Melbourne Cup draw huge crowds."]],
l:"Horse racing is a sport in which horses, usually ridden by jockeys, race against each other over a set distance. It is one of the oldest sports: chariot and mounted races were part of the ancient Olympic Games, and the Romans built huge tracks called circuses. Modern racing developed in Britain in the 17th and 18th centuries, when the Thoroughbred breed was created from Arabian stallions and English mares and the Jockey Club set rules. Flat races are run on turf or dirt over distances from about 1,000 to 4,000 meters; jump races include hurdles and fences. Racing is closely tied to betting, which funds the sport in many countries, and it raises debates about the welfare of horses.",
s:[["Ancient racing","Mounted horse races joined the ancient Olympics in 648 BC. In Rome, the Circus Maximus held perhaps 150,000 spectators for chariot races between teams with passionate fans. In Central Asia, horse races have been held for centuries, and in Mongolia’s Naadam festival, children ride in races of 15 to 30 kilometers."],["The Thoroughbred","All Thoroughbred racehorses descend from three stallions brought to England from the Middle East around 1700. They are bred for speed and stamina and can reach over 65 kilometers per hour. The Derby, first run at Epsom in 1780, gave its name to races around the world. Horses are usually raced from two or three years old."],["Racing today","Famous races include the Kentucky Derby in the United States, run since 1875, the Grand National in England, a long steeplechase over tall fences, the Melbourne Cup in Australia, the Dubai World Cup and the Prix de l’Arc de Triomphe in France. Japan and Hong Kong have huge racing industries. Concerns about injuries and the use of whips have led to new rules."]],
k:[["Breed","Thoroughbred"],["Top speed","Over 65 km/h"],["Oldest classic","St Leger, England, 1776"],["Famous US race","Kentucky Derby, since 1875"],["Festival in Mongolia","Naadam"]],
d:[["648 BC","Mounted races at the ancient Olympics"],["c.1700","Arabian stallions found the Thoroughbred breed"],["1750","The Jockey Club is founded in England"],["1780","The first Derby at Epsom"],["1839","The first Grand National"],["1875","The first Kentucky Derby"]],
s2:[["Secretariat","In 1973, the American horse Secretariat won the Triple Crown, the Kentucky Derby, Preakness Stakes and Belmont Stakes, winning the Belmont by 31 lengths in a time that still stands as a record. An autopsy found his heart was about twice the normal size."],["The Palio of Siena","Twice each summer, the Italian city of Siena holds the Palio, a bareback race of three laps around its central square, the Piazza del Campo, between riders representing the city’s districts. The race lasts about 90 seconds, and the rivalry between districts goes back centuries."]],
sp:["Jockeys","Jockeys are small and light, usually weighing around 50 to 55 kilograms, and ride with very short stirrups in a crouch over the horse’s neck. They must judge pace, position and timing in a race that lasts only a minute or two. Many jockeys suffer serious injuries during their careers."],
g:[["Jockey","A person who rides racehorses"],["Thoroughbred","A breed of horse developed for racing"],["Steeplechase","A race over fences and ditches"],["Furlong","An old distance unit of about 201 meters, still used in racing"]],
f:["All Thoroughbreds descend from three stallions.","Racehorses can run at over 65 kilometers per hour.","The Palio of Siena lasts about 90 seconds."],
fun:"The Derby’s name was decided by a coin toss between Lord Derby and Sir Charles Bunbury in 1779."});

ADDART({id:"tabletennis",t:"Table Tennis",c:"How We Live",i:"🏓",w:"Table tennis",r:["tennis","badminton","chinacountry","olymp","coldwar"],
p:[["Tennis in miniature","Table tennis is played with small bats and a light ball on a table divided by a net."],["From parlor game to Olympics","It began as an after-dinner game in Victorian England."],["China’s sport","Chinese players have dominated the sport for decades."]],
l:"Table tennis, also called ping-pong, is a sport in which two or four players hit a light hollow ball back and forth across a table divided by a low net, using small bats covered in rubber. Points are scored when an opponent fails to return the ball correctly. The game began in England in the 1880s as an indoor version of tennis played on dining tables, using books as nets and cigar-box lids as bats. Celluloid balls and rubber-covered bats transformed it into a fast sport in the 20th century. The International Table Tennis Federation was founded in 1926, and table tennis joined the Olympic Games in 1988. China has won most Olympic and world titles since the 1980s, and the sport is played by hundreds of millions of people, especially in Asia.",
s:[["Early days","Victorian families played ‘gossima’ or ‘whiff-whaff’ with improvised equipment. The name ping-pong, from the sound of the ball, was trademarked in 1901. Celluloid balls, introduced around 1900, bounced consistently, and in 1902 a player attached pimpled rubber to a wooden bat, allowing spin."],["Spin and speed","Modern bats have layers of sponge and rubber that grip the ball, letting players put tremendous topspin, backspin and sidespin on it. Balls can travel at over 100 kilometers per hour and change direction when they bounce, so matches are tests of reflexes and tactics. Games are played to 11 points, and matches to the best of five or seven games."],["China and the world","Japan dominated in the 1950s, then China from the 1960s, building a system of coaching and competition that has produced most world champions since. In 1971, ‘ping-pong diplomacy’, visits between American and Chinese players, helped open relations between the two countries. Table tennis is also one of the most popular sports for people with disabilities."]],
k:[["Origin","England, 1880s"],["Federation founded","1926"],["Olympic sport","Since 1988"],["Game","To 11 points"],["Dominant country","China"]],
d:[["1880s","Played on dining tables in England"],["1901","The name Ping-Pong is trademarked"],["1926","The first world championships"],["1971","Ping-pong diplomacy between the US and China"],["1988","Table tennis joins the Olympics"],["2000","The ball is enlarged to 40 mm to slow the game"]],
s2:[["Ping-pong diplomacy","In April 1971, the American table tennis team, competing in Japan, was invited to visit China, the first American group to enter the country in over 20 years. The visit, and a return visit by Chinese players, helped lead to President Nixon’s historic trip to China in 1972."],["Why the ball got bigger","Table tennis became so fast that spectators struggled to follow rallies. In 2000, the ball was enlarged from 38 to 40 millimeters, slowing it slightly and making rallies longer. In 2014, celluloid balls were replaced by plastic ones, which are less flammable."]],
sp:["The table","A table tennis table is 2.74 meters long, 1.525 meters wide and 76 centimeters high, with a net 15.25 centimeters tall. The surface is usually dark green or blue with white lines. Players stand well back from the table in fast rallies."],
g:[["Rally","A series of shots back and forth"],["Topspin","Spin that makes the ball dip and bounce forward"],["Serve","The shot that starts a point"],["Bat","The small racket used in table tennis, also called a paddle"]],
f:["Table tennis began on dining tables in Victorian England.","The ball can travel at over 100 kilometers per hour.","China has won most Olympic table tennis titles."],
fun:"The name ping-pong comes from the sound of the ball hitting the bat and the table."});
